@page "/components/calendar"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Calendar

<DocsHeader Title="Calendar and planner" Eyebrow="Data">
    <Lead>A month view, a week planner and an agenda that show at a glance how busy a period is and where the free time is.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    <code>StilCalendar</code>, <code>StilWeekPlanner</code> and <code>StilAgenda</code> share one data model. Like the
    <a href="components/data-grid">data grid</a>, they take any item type: <code>Items</code> plus selectors for
    <code>Start</code>, <code>End</code> and <code>Title</code>. <code>Variant</code> colors an item, for example by its
    calendar, or <code>Color</code> gives it any CSS color. <code>Details</code> adds a place or a note, and
    <code>Draft</code> marks events that are not saved yet. The rest is worked out from the items: how booked each
    working day is, the free blocks left in it, and the layout of events that overlap.
</p>
<p>
    Names, times and the first day of the week come from the current culture (or <code>Culture</code>), so a Swedish
    app gets Monday first, ISO week numbers and the 24-hour clock without settings. The working day is 08:00–17:00 on
    Monday to Friday; change it with <code>WorkdayStart</code>, <code>WorkdayEnd</code> and <code>WorkingDays</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="month">Month</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Month)">
    <p>
        Each day is tinted by how much of its working day is booked, so free and full days show without a click. A day
        shows up to <code>MaxEventsPerDay</code> events, then "+N more". In a narrow container the events become
        colored dots. Bind <code>@@bind-SelectedDate</code> for the chosen day; <code>OnDateOpen</code> fires when the
        selected day is clicked again or its "+N more" link is clicked, for example to open it in the planner.
    </p>
    <p>
        The month is a grid: the arrow keys move between the days, Home and End go to the start and end of the week, and
        moving past the first or last day shows the previous or next month. <code>Mini</code> shows dates only, and
        <code>RangeStart</code> and <code>RangeEnd</code> mark the week shown next to it.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="planner">Week planner</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Planner)">
    <p>
        <code>View</code> shows a <code>Week</code>, a <code>WorkWeek</code> or a <code>Day</code>, from
        <code>FirstHour</code> to <code>LastHour</code>. Each day has a load bar, overlapping events cascade so every
        title stays readable, free blocks are marked quietly, and today has a line at the current time.
    </p>
    <p>
        With <code>Editable</code>, events can be dragged to another time or day and resized by their bottom edge, and
        dragging on empty time selects a range. The planner reports the change in <code>OnItemChange</code> or
        <code>OnSlotSelect</code> and keeps showing your items: save the change and it redraws. A click on a free block
        also raises <code>OnSlotSelect</code>, so a new event can always be made without dragging. Open an editor from
        <code>OnItemClick</code> to change an event with the keyboard.
    </p>
    <p>
        In a container narrower than 36rem, such as a phone, the week is shown as an agenda of the same days.
        Set <code>AgendaWhenNarrow</code> to false to keep the time grid, which then scrolls sideways; the switch above
        does that.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="recurring">Recurring events</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Recurring)">
    <p>
        <code>Recurrence</code> gives an item a <code>StilRecurrence</code>: daily, weekly on given days, monthly or yearly,
        every <code>Interval</code> periods, until a date (<code>Until</code>) or for a <code>Count</code>. The item's Start
        and End are the first occurrence, and the month view, the week planner and the agenda show every one.
        <code>StilRecurrence.Parse</code> reads an iCalendar RRULE (FREQ, INTERVAL, BYDAY, UNTIL, COUNT) and
        <code>ToString</code> writes one.
    </p>
    <p>
        To cancel one occurrence, add its day to the rule's <code>Exceptions</code> (<code>Except(date)</code>). When one
        occurrence is dragged in the week planner, <code>OnItemChange</code> gets its original start in
        <code>Occurrence</code>: leave that day out of the series and add a one-off item at the new time.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="resources">Resource schedule</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Resources)">
    <p>
        <code>StilResourcePlanner</code> shows one day with <code>Resources</code> (rooms, people or vehicles; a
        <code>StilResource</code> has a <code>Key</code>, a <code>Name</code> and <code>Details</code>) as rows and the hours
        across, from <code>FirstHour</code> to <code>LastHour</code>. The <code>Resource</code> selector puts each item on
        its row; overlapping bookings share it. With <code>Editable</code>, dragging a booking raises
        <code>OnItemChange</code> with its new times and <code>Resource</code>, dragging its end resizes it, and dragging on
        an empty row raises <code>OnSlotSelect</code> with a <code>StilResourceSlot</code>. It takes the same item
        selectors as the other views, recurring items included. Set <code>--stil-resource-hour-width</code> (5rem),
        <code>--stil-resource-row-height</code> (3.5rem) and <code>--stil-resource-name-width</code> (10rem) to size it.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="agenda">Agenda</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Agenda)">
    <p>
        <code>StilAgenda</code> lists <code>Days</code> days from <code>Date</code> (today by default), with the free
        gaps of the working day between the events. It works well on small screens and as a text view next to a grid.
        Set <code>HeadingLevel</code> so the day headings fit the page's outline.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="render-modes">Render modes</h2>
<p>
    All three render in static server rendering. Give <code>StilCalendar</code> and <code>StilWeekPlanner</code> a
    <code>HrefTemplate</code>, such as <code>"planner?date={0:yyyy-MM-dd}"</code>, and their navigation becomes links.
    Selecting days, clicking events and dragging need an interactive render mode. Dragging is done by
    <code>stil.js</code>, which Stil for Blazor loads automatically. Without Blazor, the same markup works with
    <code>data-stil-editable</code> and the <code>stilweekedit</code> event; see the Stil CSS README.
</p>
<p>
    Recurring events and time zones are up to the app: expand a series into one item per occurrence, and give the
    components times in the zone they should show.
</p>

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilCalendar<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilWeekPlanner<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilResourcePlanner<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilAgenda<>)" />
